  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 206 PM MDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nTuesday through Thursday are forecast to have roughly the same \npattern, with upper ridging over the southeast and an upper trough \nover the northwest. This will keep the area in southwest flow aloft \nand provide minimal air mass change. As such, temperatures remain \nforecast to reach the 90s during the daytime, and the 60's/70s \nduring the night. Winds will also likely remain around 5-15 mph \nthrough much of the period with the low pressure lingering over the \narea. Storms will be possible each day, likely forming along the \nhigher terrain to the west and pushing east towards the area. As \nsuch, storms chances will generally favor Eastern Colorado, with \nlesser chances further east. Most storms should be sub-severe, but \na severe storm or two may be possible like the prior days.\n\nNext weekend is when there is more divergence in what may happen. \nThe currently forecast and likely scenario is that the pattern \nremains roughly the same with highs in the 90s, light winds, and \nafternoon evening storm chances. The storm chances may be a bit \nhigher as more robust shortwaves may push through with the upper \ntrough potentially deepening.\n\nThe other scenario is that the trough ejects east and pushes through \nthe Plains. With this, a strong cold front would likely push through \nFriday or Saturday and give the area a good chance for storms and \nsevere weather. Once the front passes, colder air would then filter \nin from the north and likely give us a break from the 90s. Storm \nchances would also likely be minimal with the drier and cooler air \nover the area. This scenario currently has a 25-35% chance of \noccurring.",
